      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4 t + \\sin {t},\\,y=t + \\cos {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\cos {t} + 4,\\,y'(t)=1 - \\sin {t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(1 - \\sin {t}\\right)}{\\left(\\cos {t} + 4\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1 - \\sin {t}}{\\cos {t} + 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1 - \\sin {\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}}{\\cos {\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} + 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1 - \\left(1\\right)}{\\left(0\\right) + 4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
