      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=6 \\cos {2 t},\\,y'(t)=4 \\sin {t} \\cos {t}=2 \\sin {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(2 \\sin {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(6 \\cos {2 t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\tan {2 t}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\tan {2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
